<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Ancón</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1883</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Ancón, signed on 20 October 1883, ended the war between Chile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a>. Peru ceded the nitrate province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a> to Chile, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a> were to remain under Chilean control for ten years, followed by a plebiscite. The plebiscite was never held, and the dispute was settled only by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> of 1929.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ancon.jpg" alt="Treaty of Ancón" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Janitoalevic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Anc%C3%B3n.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
